<h2>How burst pipe claims are usually covered</h2>

<p>Most homeowners and commercial property policies treat water damage differently depending on its source. A pipe that suddenly splits from freezing and sprays water into walls and floors is commonly handled as a <strong>sudden and accidental discharge</strong> of water from a plumbing system. That is different from flood (surface water from outside, which standard policies generally exclude) and from long-term leaks or seepage (often excluded). Our guide to <a href="/water-damage-vs-flood-damage-mcallen/">water damage vs. flood damage</a> explains those distinctions.</p>

<h3>The freezing exclusion and its conditions</h3>

<p>Many policies include a provision addressing freezing of plumbing, heating, air conditioning, or fire sprinkler systems. A common approach is that the policy does not pay for freeze damage <em>unless</em> you used reasonable care to:</p>
<ul>
<li>maintain heat in the building, <strong>or</strong></li>
<li>shut off the water supply and drain the system and appliances.</li>
</ul>

<p>Some policies apply this condition only while the property is vacant, unoccupied, or under construction. Others apply it more broadly. The exact wording matters a great deal, so read your own policy rather than relying on a general rule. During February 2021, widespread power outages across Texas complicated "maintaining heat" questions. If your power was out, keep anything that documents the outage, such as utility notices, texts, or photos.</p>

<h3>Vacant and unoccupied properties</h3>

<p>Rental homes between tenants, seasonal homes owned by Winter Texans, and commercial buildings closed for a period can face extra scrutiny. Policies often define "vacant" and "unoccupied" differently and may limit or exclude certain losses after a property has been vacant for a set period. If you own a property that sits empty part of the year, it is worth reviewing those definitions before the next cold snap.</p>

