<h2>Commonly disputed costs on freeze claims</h2>

<p>Even when coverage is accepted, the scope and price of repairs often become the main disagreement.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost item</th><th>Why it gets disputed</th><th>What helps</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Tear-out and access</td><td>Policies often pay to open walls, ceilings, or slabs to reach the broken pipe, but disputes arise over how much needed to be removed.</td><td>Plumber notes, moisture readings, and photos showing where water traveled.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The pipe or fixture itself</td><td>Many policies cover resulting damage but not repair of the failed pipe.</td><td>Separate plumbing invoice lines for the repair vs. access and restoration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water mitigation (drying)</td><td>Equipment days, number of dehumidifiers, and removal of wet materials.</td><td>Daily moisture logs and drying reports from the mitigation company.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Matching finishes</td><td>Flooring, cabinets, and wall finishes that run continuously through a home.</td><td>Photos showing continuous flooring and discontinued products.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mold remediation</td><td>Often excluded or subject to a low sublimit.</td><td>Proof of prompt mitigation; policy mold endorsement wording.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional living expenses</td><td>How long the home was unlivable.</td><td>Receipts and a clear repair timeline.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Tear-out and access costs</h3>

<p>In RGV homes, supply lines often run through attics or exterior walls, and some run under slabs. When a line splits in an attic, water can soak insulation, drywall ceilings, and walls below. Reaching and repairing the break may require removing ceiling drywall, insulation, cabinets, or flooring. Many policies cover reasonable tear-out needed to access the system, then restoration of what was removed. Make sure the estimate includes both steps.</p>

<h3>Mold limits</h3>

<p>Water left in walls and under flooring in South Texas humidity can grow mold quickly. Many Texas policies exclude mold or limit coverage to a modest amount, sometimes only when it results from a covered water loss. The practical lesson: start drying immediately. Delays can shift part of the loss outside coverage.</p>

<h2>Mitigation steps right after a pipe bursts</h2>

<p>Your policy generally requires you to take reasonable steps to protect the property from further damage. That also happens to be the best way to keep a manageable loss from becoming a large one.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Shut off the water</strong> at the main valve. Know where it is before a freeze.</li>
<li><strong>Turn off electricity</strong> to affected areas if water is near outlets, fixtures, or panels, and if it is safe to do so.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything</strong> before cleanup, including the broken pipe, standing water, and damaged contents.</li>
<li><strong>Call a plumber</strong> to repair the break, and keep the failed section of pipe if possible.</li>
<li><strong>Start drying.</strong> Remove standing water and call a water mitigation company if materials are soaked.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and write down your claim number and adjuster's contact information.</li>
<li><strong>Keep receipts</strong> for emergency repairs, equipment, hotel stays, and meals if you had to leave.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-gold"><span class="callout-title">Before the next freeze</span><p>The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/safety/winter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service winter safety guidance</a> and local forecasts from <a href="https://www.weather.gov/bro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NWS Brownsville</a> give advance notice of hard freezes. Insulate exposed lines, cover outdoor faucets, let faucets drip during extended freezes, and know how to shut off your water. Check our <a href="/weather/">live McAllen weather</a> page when a cold front is coming.</p></div>

<h2>Documentation that supports a freeze claim</h2>

<p>Freeze claims can be documented more cleanly than many storm claims because the failure is usually visible and the timing is known. Strong files typically include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Dated photos and video of the split pipe, the water spread, and every affected room.</li>
<li>A plumber's invoice describing the location and cause of the failure (for example, a freeze split in an attic supply line).</li>
<li>Mitigation company records: moisture maps, daily readings, and equipment logs.</li>
<li>An inventory of damaged contents with ages, descriptions, and replacement prices.</li>
<li>Records showing heat was maintained or the water was shut off, such as utility bills, thermostat history, or outage notices.</li>
<li>NOAA records for the freeze dates, which confirm when freezing temperatures occurred.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> includes a water damage section you can print or save. If your policy pays replacement cost, the difference between the first payment and the full amount may depend on depreciation; see <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>When a public adjuster may help</h2>

<p>Freeze losses can look simple at first, then grow once walls and floors are opened. If an insurer's estimate leaves out tear-out, drying, matching, or contents, or if a freeze exclusion is being applied in a way you do not understand, a licensed Texas public adjuster can review the policy, document the full scope, and present the claim. Learn more on our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> pages. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If coverage is denied outright and you are considering legal action, talk to a Texas attorney about your options and deadlines.</p>

